@page "/sources"

<PageTitle>Sources &amp; Methods | Nuclear Evaluation</PageTitle>

<div class="sources-page">
    <header class="sources-header">
        <h1>Sources &amp; Methods</h1>
        <p>Where the fleet and emissions figures come from and how they differ from the workspace data.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="sources-published">
        <section class="sources-card" aria-labelledby="sources-fleet-title">
            <header class="sources-card-header">
                <span class="sources-icon" aria-hidden="true"><RadzenIcon Icon="map" /></span>
                <h2 id="sources-fleet-title">U.S. nuclear fleet</h2>
                <p>The 2024 snapshot covers nuclear generators that the U.S. Energy Information Administration lists as operating at year-end.</p>
            </header>

            <dl class="sources-definitions">
                <div>
                    <dt>Net summer capacity <span>MW</span></dt>
                    <dd>Maximum power a generator can supply during summer peak demand, after accounting for power used by the plant.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Net generation <span>MWh or GWh</span></dt>
                    <dd>Electricity generated over time, after subtracting the electricity used by the plant. A gigawatt-hour is 1,000 megawatt-hours.</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>

            <p class="sources-method">
                Plant-month totals add the reported values for included reactors. A month with no reported values stays blank.
                Negative values are preserved, and an annual plant total requires 12 reported months.
                The Generation Calendar divides the reported state total by the number of days in the month for its daily average.
            </p>

            <ul class="sources-references" aria-label="Fleet datasets" role="list">
                <li>
                    <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/data/eia860/archive/xls/eia8602024.zip">
                        EIA-860 generator inventory <span>2024 ZIP</span>
                    </a>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="https://www.eia.gov/nuclear/generation/archive/xls/usreact24.xlsx">
                        EIA reactor generation workbook <span>2024 XLSX</span>
                    </a>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p class="sources-map-note">
                State outlines use U.S. Census cartographic boundaries distributed by
                <a href="https://github.com/topojson/us-atlas" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Atlas</a>.
                <a href="data/fleet-map-license.txt">Map license</a>
            </p>
            <div class="sources-actions">
                <a class="sources-action" href="fleet">Explore the fleet <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                <a class="sources-action" href="fleet-generation">Open the generation calendar <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="sources-card" aria-labelledby="sources-emissions-title">
            <header class="sources-card-header">
                <span class="sources-icon" aria-hidden="true"><RadzenIcon Icon="stacked_bar_chart" /></span>
                <h2 id="sources-emissions-title">Lifecycle emissions</h2>
                <p>Energy &amp; Emissions compares six technologies using published greenhouse gas estimates.</p>
            </header>

            <dl class="sources-definitions">
                <div>
                    <dt>Emissions intensity <span>g CO₂e/kWh</span></dt>
                    <dd>Grams of carbon dioxide equivalent for each kilowatt-hour of electricity generated over a technology's lifecycle.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Lifecycle phases</dt>
                    <dd>Materials and construction, combustion where applicable, other operating activities, and end of life.</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>

            <p class="sources-method">
                The published phase and lifecycle medians are calculated separately, so the phase values may add to a different total.
                The calculator multiplies the lifecycle median by the electricity generated to estimate a hypothetical total.
            </p>

            <ul class="sources-references" aria-label="Emissions source" role="list">
                <li>
                    <a href="https://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy21osti/80580.pdf#page=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                        NREL lifecycle assessment, Table 1 <span>2021 PDF</span>
                    </a>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="sources-actions">
                <a class="sources-action" href="energy-emissions#emissions-estimate">Compare emissions <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>

    <section class="sources-card sources-workspace" aria-labelledby="sources-workspace-title">
